1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:07,881
One line of SQL took this database from 26 order lookups a second to nearly 37,000.

2
00:00:08,331 --> 00:00:15,157
The test: 2 million orders in Postgres. The My Orders page asks for one customer's latest 20.

3
00:00:15,607 --> 00:00:23,223
With no index, Postgres reads every row. About 2 million checked to return 20 rows: 43 ms.

4
00:00:23,673 --> 00:00:27,428
With 4 users at once: 26 lookups a second.

5
00:00:27,878 --> 00:00:34,406
The fix: one index. The column the page filters by, then the sort order. Built in under a second.

6
00:00:34,856 --> 00:00:41,469
Same query: under a tenth of a millisecond. Same load test: about 37,000 a second.

7
00:00:41,919 --> 00:00:49,109
The catch: every index is updated on every write. So index what your slow queries need, not every column.

8
00:00:49,559 --> 00:00:53,996
